WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.830
Suppose you keep a phone book

2
00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.030
and you need two operations: look a name up

3
00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:09.940
and add a new name.

4
00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.260
If you keep the names in a sorted array

5
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.200
lookup is fast.

6
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.071
You can use binary search

7
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.104
and find any name in about

8
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.714
log base two of n steps.

9
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.264
But adding a name means shifting

10
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.040
everyone after it

11
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.210
which takes up to n steps.

12
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.490
If you keep them in a linked list

13
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.850
adding is fast

14
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:34.030
but finding a name means walking the list

15
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.880
which takes up to n steps.

16
00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.310
Can we get both operations fast at once?

17
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.770
The answer is the binary search tree.

18
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.975
In this video we'll see the

19
00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.290
rule that defines it

20
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.850
how to search for a key

21
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.600
and how to insert one.

22
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.187
A binary search tree is a binary

23
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.230
tree with one extra rule.

24
00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:55.397
For every node

25
00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.272
all the keys in its left subtree are

26
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.011
smaller than the node's key

27
00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.694
and all the keys in its right

28
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.380
subtree are larger.

29
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.340
Here is an example with seven keys.

30
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.960
Look at fifty at the root.

31
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.320
Everything on its left is smaller, thirty

32
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.540
twenty and forty.

33
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.040
Everything on its right is larger, seventy

34
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.420
sixty and eighty.

35
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.880
And the same rule holds again at thirty

36
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.610
and at seventy.

37
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.794
Notice the words all the

38
00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.184
keys in the subtree.

39
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.971
It is not enough to compare a

40
00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.440
node with its two children.

41
00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.180
Look at this tree.

42
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.920
Thirty is smaller than fifty, fine.

43
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.100
Sixty is larger than thirty, also fine.

44
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.680
But sixty sits in the left subtree of fifty

45
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.260
and sixty is larger than fifty.

46
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.900
Every parent and child pair looks correct

47
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.990
and the tree is still broken.

48
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.850
Remember the inorder walk

49
00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.129
from the last video.

50
00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:53.502
In a correct search tree it visits

51
00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.450
the keys in sorted order: twenty, thirty

52
00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.178
forty, fifty, sixty, seventy, eighty.

53
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.577
The rule and the sorted walk are

54
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.580
two views of the same property.

55
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.630
Now we can search.

56
00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.550
To look for sixty, start at the root.

57
00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.490
Sixty is larger than fifty

58
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.305
so sixty can only be in the right subtree

59
00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.276
and the whole left half of the tree

60
00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.360
is ruled out in one comparison.

61
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.160
Move to seventy.

62
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.780
Sixty is smaller, so go left.

63
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.660
We arrive at sixty.

64
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.350
Found, after three comparisons.

65
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.690
Now look for forty-five

66
00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:31.390
which is not in the tree.

67
00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:33.750
Fifty: go left.

68
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.510
Thirty: forty-five is larger, so go right.

69
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.370
Forty: larger again, go right.

70
00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.380
And the right child of forty is empty.

71
00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.400
Reaching an empty spot means

72
00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:46.980
the key is not here.

73
00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.699
And notice something useful

74
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.891
the empty spot is exactly where

75
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.010
forty-five would have to be.

76
00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.900
Insertion is the same walk.

77
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.460
To insert forty-five, search for it.

78
00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.430
We fall off the tree at the right of forty.

79
00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.570
So that is where the new node goes

80
00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.690
a new leaf, the right child of forty.

81
00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:08.970
Nothing else moves

82
00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.420
and no other node has to be touched.

83
00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.540
The rule still holds.

84
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.580
Forty-five is larger than thirty

85
00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.900
smaller than fifty, larger than forty.

86
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.780
And the inorder walk still comes out sorted

87
00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.810
with forty-five between forty and fifty.

88
00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.550
One more detail.

89
00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.690
If the key is already in the tree

90
00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.190
we stop and do nothing

91
00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.480
because a search tree stores each key once.

92
00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.070
How long does this take?

93
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.374
Every step goes one level down the tree

94
00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:39.031
and we do a constant amount

95
00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.580
of work per level.

96
00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.703
So the cost is proportional

97
00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.220
to the height of the tree.

98
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.028
If we call the height h

99
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.726
search and insert both take at

100
00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.970
most h plus one comparisons: time O of h.

101
00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.790
For this tree, h is two

102
00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:56.890
then three after the insertion.

103
00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.510
If a tree with n keys is nicely balanced

104
00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.090
its height is about log base two of n

105
00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.680
and then we are as fast as binary search.

106
00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.851
But height is not guaranteed to be small

107
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.627
and that is the question for the

108
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.230
last video of this chapter.

109
00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.123
Back to the phone book.

110
00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.186
A sorted array finds in log

111
00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.654
n but inserts in n.

112
00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:21.954
A linked list inserts in constant

113
00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:24.330
time but finds in n.

114
00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.278
The search tree finds and inserts in

115
00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.570
time proportional to its height

116
00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.850
and removing a key, which is next

117
00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:32.970
costs the same.

118
00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.790
When the tree is balanced

119
00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.160
that is log n for all of them.

120
00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:38.750
To sum up.

121
00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.308
In a binary search tree

122
00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.851
every key in the left subtree is smaller and

123
00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:46.312
every key in the right subtree is larger

124
00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.704
and the inorder walk visits

125
00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.940
the keys in sorted order.

126
00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.000
Search, and insert

127
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.920
follow one path from the root

128
00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.580
and cost time proportional to the height.

129
00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.570
A new key is always attached as a leaf.

130
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.400
Next: what happens when we delete a key.

131
00:05:05.027 --> 00:05:07.547
Now let's write a search tree in Java.

132
00:05:08.127 --> 00:05:10.141
The class is generic

133
00:05:10.237 --> 00:05:12.581
K extends Comparable of K means

134
00:05:12.657 --> 00:05:14.677
that any key type works

135
00:05:14.677 --> 00:05:17.377
as long as two keys can be compared.

136
00:05:17.377 --> 00:05:19.447
Integers and strings already can.

137
00:05:19.797 --> 00:05:22.467
The tree holds the root, the size

138
00:05:22.467 --> 00:05:24.547
and a counter of comparisons.

139
00:05:24.547 --> 00:05:27.237
An empty tree has a root of null.

140
00:05:27.817 --> 00:05:29.187
Search is a loop.

141
00:05:29.187 --> 00:05:31.547
We call compare to on the key.

142
00:05:31.547 --> 00:05:33.147
Zero means a match.

143
00:05:33.147 --> 00:05:35.687
A negative number means the key is smaller

144
00:05:35.687 --> 00:05:38.537
so we go left; otherwise we go right.

145
00:05:38.887 --> 00:05:41.397
Fall off the tree, and node is null

146
00:05:41.397 --> 00:05:43.407
so we return false.

147
00:05:43.987 --> 00:05:46.257
To return two things at once

148
00:05:46.257 --> 00:05:48.857
the path and whether the key was found

149
00:05:48.857 --> 00:05:50.255
we use a record.

150
00:05:50.337 --> 00:05:52.519
It is a short way to declare

151
00:05:52.597 --> 00:05:54.907
a small immutable class.

152
00:05:55.257 --> 00:06:00.267
Sixty visits fifty, seventy, sixty.

153
00:06:00.267 --> 00:06:04.567
Forty-five visits fifty, thirty

154
00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:07.177
forty and falls off.

155
00:06:07.757 --> 00:06:09.827
Insert walks the same path.

156
00:06:09.827 --> 00:06:11.887
Zero means the key is already there

157
00:06:11.887 --> 00:06:13.107
so return false.

158
00:06:13.107 --> 00:06:16.007
If the child on the correct side is null

159
00:06:16.007 --> 00:06:17.567
we attach a new node there

160
00:06:17.567 --> 00:06:19.117
and increase the size.

161
00:06:19.697 --> 00:06:21.947
After inserting the seven keys

162
00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:24.287
the inorder list is sorted.

163
00:06:24.287 --> 00:06:27.183
Inserting forty-five makes the height three

164
00:06:27.247 --> 00:06:28.906
and the minimum and maximum

165
00:06:28.967 --> 00:06:30.657
are twenty and eighty.

166
00:06:31.237 --> 00:06:33.510
Let's follow the insertion of forty-five

167
00:06:33.567 --> 00:06:35.447
with the comparison counter.

168
00:06:35.447 --> 00:06:38.967
Compare with fifty: smaller, so go left.

169
00:06:38.967 --> 00:06:42.827
Compare with thirty: larger, so go right.

170
00:06:42.827 --> 00:06:46.587
Compare with forty: larger, so go right

171
00:06:46.587 --> 00:06:48.227
and that child is null

172
00:06:48.227 --> 00:06:49.957
so the new node goes there.

173
00:06:50.307 --> 00:06:53.250
Three comparisons, for a tree of height two.

174
00:06:53.317 --> 00:06:55.965
In general an insertion makes at most

175
00:06:56.037 --> 00:06:57.857
height plus one comparisons

176
00:06:57.857 --> 00:07:00.187
and the demo prints exactly three.

177
00:07:00.537 --> 00:07:03.431
This counter is the cost we will watch

178
00:07:03.507 --> 00:07:05.777
in the last video of this chapter.

179
00:07:06.357 --> 00:07:08.787
The ordering check passes down bounds

180
00:07:08.787 --> 00:07:10.646
null meaning no bound.

181
00:07:10.727 --> 00:07:12.884
Every key must be strictly between

182
00:07:12.947 --> 00:07:14.317
the keys above it.

183
00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:16.571
The tests call it after every

184
00:07:16.637 --> 00:07:18.147
insert and delete.

185
00:07:18.727 --> 00:07:20.277
Common mistakes.

186
00:07:20.277 --> 00:07:21.117
First

187
00:07:21.117 --> 00:07:24.167
comparing a node only with its parent.

188
00:07:24.517 --> 00:07:27.594
Second, a key type whose compare-to method

189
00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:30.001
does not agree with equals.

190
00:07:30.087 --> 00:07:33.154
Then the tree and a hash set will disagree

191
00:07:33.227 --> 00:07:35.157
about which keys are the same.

192
00:07:35.507 --> 00:07:37.392
Third, a null key

193
00:07:37.497 --> 00:07:39.107
which makes the comparison throw

194
00:07:39.157 --> 00:07:40.867
a NullPointerException.

195
00:07:41.447 --> 00:07:43.303
In practice you would not

196
00:07:43.377 --> 00:07:44.897
write this yourself.

197
00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:47.917
TreeMap and TreeSet are search trees

198
00:07:47.917 --> 00:07:50.037
kept balanced as red-black trees

199
00:07:50.037 --> 00:07:52.577
and they offer floor, ceiling

200
00:07:52.577 --> 00:07:54.047
and ordered iteration.

201
00:07:54.397 --> 00:07:56.344
Writing our own is how we learn

202
00:07:56.407 --> 00:07:57.887
why they stay fast

203
00:07:57.887 --> 00:07:59.917
which is the subject of Chapter six.

204
00:08:00.497 --> 00:08:01.607
In Java

205
00:08:01.607 --> 00:08:04.087
a generic class with comparable keys

206
00:08:04.087 --> 00:08:06.687
loops for search and insert

207
00:08:06.687 --> 00:08:08.727
a record for the path

208
00:08:08.727 --> 00:08:11.537
and an ordering check for the invariant.

209
00:08:11.887 --> 00:08:14.207
Next: deleting keys.

